        durationMinutes: 45,
        sequenceOrder: 3,
        contentType: 'TEXT',
        contentUrl: null,
        content: "MENU PLANNING, NUTRITION & SPECIAL DIETARY REQUIREMENTS\n\n1. Nutrition for FIFO Mining and Heavy Industrial Workforces\n- FIFO mine site personnel perform demanding 12-hour shifts, often in extreme heat and dusty conditions.\n- Nutritional Requirements:\n  * High complex carbohydrates (brown rice, oats, sweet potatoes) for sustained muscle glycogen replenishment.\n  * Lean proteins (chicken, beef, eggs, legumes) for tissue repair.\n  * Healthy unsaturated fats and high-fiber vegetables.\n  * Abundant hydration support and electrolyte replenishment; low-sodium, heart-healthy options.\n\n2. Special Dietary Requirements and Lifestyle Diets\n- Vegetarian & Vegan Diets: Complete exclusion of meat/seafood (vegetarian), and all animal products including dairy, eggs, and honey (vegan). Dedicated utensils and cooking surfaces must be used.\n- Religious Diets:\n  * Halal: Permissible under Islamic law; strict prohibition of pork and alcohol in all food preparations; meat must be certified Halal slaughtered.\n  * Kosher: Conforming to Jewish dietary laws (kashrut); separation of meat and dairy; certified slaughter.\n\n3. The Mandatory Declarable Food Allergens (FSANZ Standard 1.2.3)\nUnder the Australia New Zealand Food Standards Code, food businesses MUST accurately declare the presence of major food allergens:\n- Peanuts\n- Tree Nuts (almonds, cashews, walnuts, macadamias, pecans, pistachios, hazelnuts, etc.)\n- Milk and Dairy products\n- Eggs\n- Sesame Seeds\n- Fish\n- Crustaceans (prawns, crab, lobster)\n- Molluscs (mussels, oysters, squid, clams)\n- Soybeans and soy products\n- Cereals containing Gluten (wheat, barley, rye, oats)\n- Lupin\n- Added Sulphites (in concentrations of 10 mg/kg or more).\n- Severe Allergy Warning: Ingestion of minute traces of allergens can trigger fatal anaphylaxis in sensitized individuals. If unsure of an ingredient's allergen status, NEVER GUESS \u2014 always verify the manufacturer label or ask the Food Safety Supervisor."
